window.QUIZ_BANK['selenium'] = (window.QUIZ_BANK['selenium']||[]).concat([
{id:3971,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is Selenium WebDriver?',options:['A browser plugin','An open-source framework providing a programming interface to control web browsers programmatically for automated testing','A load testing tool','A mock server'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium WebDriver drives browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari) directly via native browser automation APIs. It follows the W3C WebDriver specification. Used for functional UI testing of web applications.'},
{id:3972,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the W3C WebDriver specification?',options:['A JavaScript standard','An international standard (W3C) defining a platform-neutral HTTP API for controlling web browsers, ensuring consistent driver behavior across browser vendors','A CSS testing standard','A JSON format'],answer:1,explanation:'All modern browser drivers (ChromeDriver, GeckoDriver, EdgeDriver) implement the W3C WebDriver standard. This ensures Selenium commands work consistently across browsers without browser-specific hacks.'},
{id:3973,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does `driver.get("https://example.com")` do?',options:['Returns a string','Navigates the browser to the specified URL, waiting for the page load event to fire before returning','Opens a new tab','Creates a new driver'],answer:1,explanation:'driver.get() loads the URL and waits until the browser fires the document.readyState === "complete" event. It is blocking — control returns only when the page load is complete (or timeout occurs).'},
{id:3974,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the difference between `driver.close()` and `driver.quit()`?',options:['They are identical','close() closes only the current browser window/tab; quit() closes ALL windows and terminates the WebDriver session and browser process','quit() only closes one tab','close() terminates the session'],answer:1,explanation:'close(): closes current window, session remains if other windows open. quit(): ends the WebDriver session, closes all windows, and kills the browser process. Always call quit() in teardown to prevent orphaned browser processes.'},
{id:3975,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is ChromeDriver?',options:['A Chrome extension','A standalone server implementing the W3C WebDriver standard for Chrome, acting as the bridge between Selenium and the Chrome browser','A Chrome plugin API','A JavaScript driver'],answer:1,explanation:'ChromeDriver must match the installed Chrome version. It listens on an HTTP port for WebDriver commands and translates them to native Chrome DevTools Protocol commands. WebDriverManager can auto-download the correct version.'},
{id:3976,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What are the Selenium locator strategies via `By` class?',options:['xpath only','By.id, By.name, By.className, By.tagName, By.linkText, By.partialLinkText, By.cssSelector, By.xpath','id and xpath only','CSS and id only'],answer:1,explanation:'Eight strategies: id (unique, fastest), name (form fields), className (CSS class), tagName (HTML tag), linkText (exact anchor text), partialLinkText (partial anchor text), cssSelector (powerful), xpath (most flexible).'},
{id:3977,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does `driver.findElement(By.id("username"))` return?',options:['A String containing the value','A WebElement representing the first element matching the locator, or throws NoSuchElementException if not found','A list of elements','A boolean'],answer:1,explanation:'findElement returns a single WebElement (the first match). If not found: NoSuchElementException. For multiple elements: findElements() returns List<WebElement> (empty list if none found, no exception).'},
{id:3978,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does `element.sendKeys("text")` do?',options:['Returns the element text','Types the specified text into the focused element (typically an input field), character by character, triggering keyboard events','Passes data to an API','Sets the value attribute directly'],answer:1,explanation:'sendKeys simulates keyboard input: it triggers keydown, keypress, keyup events for each character. Works on input, textarea, contenteditable elements. Also accepts Keys enum: sendKeys(Keys.RETURN), sendKeys(Keys.chord(Keys.CONTROL,"a")).'},
{id:3979,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does `element.getText()` return?',options:['The element\'s HTML','The visible inner text of an element, equivalent to the innerText DOM property — excluding hidden text','The value attribute','The element ID'],answer:1,explanation:'getText() returns the rendered visible text, stripping HTML tags. It reflects what the user sees. For input values: getAttribute("value"). For hidden elements: getText() returns an empty string.'},
{id:3980,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is `element.getAttribute("name")` used for?',options:['Gets the element name only','Returns the value of an HTML attribute (e.g., value, href, class, placeholder, disabled). Returns null if the attribute does not exist.','Gets the element type','Returns the CSS property'],answer:1,explanation:'getAttribute("value") gets input field content, getAttribute("href") gets link URL, getAttribute("class") gets CSS classes. Selenium 4 also has getDomAttribute() for exact attribute value vs property.'},
{id:3981,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is implicit wait in Selenium?',options:['A Thread.sleep()','A global configuration telling WebDriver to wait up to N seconds when trying to locate elements before throwing NoSuchElementException: driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(Duration.ofSeconds(10))','An explicit wait condition','A page load timeout'],answer:1,explanation:'Implicit wait: applies to all findElement/findElements calls globally. Polls the DOM until element is found or timeout. Mixes poorly with explicit waits — can cause unexpected delays. Set once, avoid combining with explicit waits.'},
{id:3982,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is explicit wait in Selenium?',options:['Thread.sleep() equivalent','A WebDriverWait that waits for a specific condition to be true before proceeding: new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10)).until(ExpectedConditions.visibilityOf(element))','A global wait setting','An implicit wait upgrade'],answer:1,explanation:'Explicit waits are condition-specific and more reliable than implicit waits. WebDriverWait polls every 500ms (default) until the condition passes or timeout. Always prefer explicit waits for dynamic elements.'},
{id:3983,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is FluentWait in Selenium?',options:['A water-flowing animation wait','A customizable wait that allows setting polling frequency, timeout, and exceptions to ignore: new FluentWait<>(driver).withTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30)).pollingEvery(Duration.ofSeconds(2)).ignoring(NoSuchElementException.class)','A fluid animation helper','An async wait'],answer:1,explanation:'FluentWait offers fine-grained control: custom polling interval, custom timeout, ignore specific exceptions, custom message. WebDriverWait extends FluentWait. Use for elements that appear intermittently or require custom polling.'},
{id:3984,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What are common ExpectedConditions in Selenium?',options:['Only visibilityOf','visibilityOfElementLocated, elementToBeClickable, presenceOfElementLocated, invisibilityOfElementLocated, textToBePresentInElement, titleContains, urlContains, alertIsPresent, frameToBeAvailableAndSwitchToIt','Only presenceOf and visibility','Custom conditions only'],answer:1,explanation:'Key ExpectedConditions: visibilityOfElementLocated (visible + present in DOM), elementToBeClickable (visible + enabled), presenceOfElementLocated (in DOM, not necessarily visible), invisibilityOf (not visible or not in DOM).'},
{id:3985,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Page Object Model (POM) in Selenium?',options:['A Maven dependency','A design pattern where each web page is represented as a class with locators as fields and user interactions as methods, separating test logic from page structure','A test framework','A locator strategy'],answer:1,explanation:'POM: LoginPage class with username, password, loginButton fields and login(user, pass) method. Tests use: loginPage.login("admin","pass") not driver.findElement(By.id("user")).sendKeys("admin"). Improves maintainability.'},
{id:3986,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `PageFactory.initElements()` in Selenium?',options:['A factory method for drivers','Initializes WebElement fields annotated with @FindBy in a Page Object class, creating lazy proxies that locate elements at the time of access','A driver factory','A test factory'],answer:1,explanation:'PageFactory.initElements(driver, this) in the Page Object constructor initializes @FindBy annotated fields. Elements are found lazily on first access. @CacheLookup caches the element after first find (risky for dynamic pages).'},
{id:3987,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does `@FindBy(id="username")` do in PageFactory?',options:['A CSS annotation','Annotates a WebElement field in a PageFactory-based page object, specifying the locator strategy and value to find the element when accessed','A TestNG annotation','A test field marker'],answer:1,explanation:'@FindBy(id="username") WebElement usernameField — creates a proxy. On usernameField.sendKeys(), PageFactory finds the element by id="username". Supports: id, name, className, tagName, linkText, css, xpath.'},
{id:3988,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the `@FindBys` annotation in PageFactory?',options:['Multiple locators (AND logic)','Finds an element matching ALL the specified @FindBy locators (AND condition) — element must match all criteria','Multiple locators (OR logic)','A compound selector'],answer:1,explanation:'@FindBys({@FindBy(className="form"), @FindBy(id="btn")}) finds elements matching BOTH conditions (AND). Use @FindAll for OR logic. Useful for narrowing down elements in complex DOM structures.'},
{id:3989,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `driver.navigate()` in Selenium?',options:['Only for URL navigation','Provides browser navigation: navigate().to(url), navigate().back(), navigate().forward(), navigate().refresh() — different from get() as back/forward/refresh are only on navigate()','A routing API','A tab manager'],answer:1,explanation:'navigate().to() is equivalent to get() but through the navigation interface. navigate().back() simulates browser back button. navigate().refresh() reloads. navigate().forward() goes forward in history.'},
{id:3990,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `driver.manage().window()` used for?',options:['Opens a new window','Controls the browser window: maximize(), fullscreen(), minimize(), setSize(dimension), setPosition(point), getSize(), getPosition()','Sets the viewport','Changes the browser zoom'],answer:1,explanation:'driver.manage().window().maximize() is standard test setup. setSize(new Dimension(1280,800)) for specific viewport testing. getSize() for responsive testing assertions. Available after driver initialization.'},
{id:3991,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What causes a `StaleElementReferenceException` in Selenium?',options:['Element not found','The element was in the DOM when found but the DOM was refreshed or the element was re-rendered, making the old reference invalid','A timeout error','A null pointer'],answer:1,explanation:'StaleElementReference: page loaded a new DOM after element was found (AJAX updates, navigation, JS re-render). Solution: re-find the element before interacting. Use try/catch with retry, or ExpectedConditions.refreshed().'},
{id:3992,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you handle a StaleElementReferenceException?',options:['Catch and ignore it','Re-find the element using findElement() again before interaction, or use explicit wait with ExpectedConditions.refreshed(element) which retries until the element is fresh','Use implicit wait only','Use a static element reference'],answer:1,explanation:'WebElement element = wait.until(ExpectedConditions.refreshed(ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(By.id("btn")))). Or: try{ el.click(); }catch(StaleElementReferenceException e){ driver.findElement(locator).click(); }'},
{id:3993,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `JavascriptExecutor` in Selenium?',options:['A JavaScript linter','An interface enabling execution of JavaScript in the browser context: ((JavascriptExecutor)driver).executeScript("return document.title;") or executing arbitrary JS','A browser console','A script runner'],answer:1,explanation:'JavascriptExecutor uses: scroll: executeScript("window.scrollTo(0,500)"); click hidden element: executeScript("arguments[0].click();", element); get value: executeScript("return arguments[0].value;", el); highlight: executeScript("arguments[0].style.border=\'3px solid red\';",el).'},
{id:3994,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `executeAsyncScript` in Selenium?',options:['Runs JS asynchronously in a thread','Executes JavaScript that signals completion by calling the callback passed as the last argument, useful for async JS operations','Runs JS in a service worker','A Promise executor'],answer:1,explanation:'executeAsyncScript("var callback=arguments[arguments.length-1]; setTimeout(callback, 2000);") waits for the callback. scriptTimeout must accommodate the async operation duration.'},
{id:3995,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the `Actions` class in Selenium?',options:['A utility class for test actions','Provides advanced user interaction simulation: hover (moveToElement), drag-and-drop, right-click (contextClick), double-click, key combinations (keyDown+sendKeys+keyUp), click and hold','A keyboard simulator only','A touch action class'],answer:1,explanation:'Actions actions = new Actions(driver); actions.moveToElement(menu).click(item).perform() — method chaining. perform() executes all built actions. Essential for complex interactions like drag-and-drop, hover menus, and keyboard shortcuts.'},
{id:3996,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does `actions.moveToElement(element).perform()` do?',options:['Scrolls to the element','Moves the mouse cursor to the center of the element (hover), triggering mouseover/mouseenter events — used for dropdown menus and tooltips that require hover','Clicks the element','Focuses the element'],answer:1,explanation:'moveToElement triggers CSS :hover state and mouseover events. Required for dropdown menus that only appear on hover. Followed by clicking a sub-menu item: actions.moveToElement(menu).moveToElement(subItem).click().perform()'},
{id:3997,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `actions.dragAndDrop(source, target).perform()`?',options:['Copy-paste operation','Drags the source element and drops it on the target element, simulating the full drag-and-drop interaction with mousedown, mousemove, mouseup events','A scroll interaction','A focus transfer'],answer:1,explanation:'actions.dragAndDrop(draggable, droppable).perform() handles HTML5 drag-and-drop. For complex cases, use: actions.clickAndHold(source).moveToElement(target).release().perform() or JavaScript-based drag-and-drop for React/Vue components.'},
{id:3998,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the `Select` class in Selenium?',options:['A CSS selector utility','A class that wraps <select> elements for dropdown interaction: selectByValue, selectByVisibleText, selectByIndex, getOptions, getAllSelectedOptions, isMultiple','A query selector','A find utility'],answer:1,explanation:'Select select = new Select(driver.findElement(By.id("country"))); select.selectByVisibleText("USA"); select.selectByValue("us"); List<WebElement> opts = select.getOptions(). Only works with HTML <select> elements.'},
{id:3999,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you handle browser alerts in Selenium?',options:['They are handled automatically','Use driver.switchTo().alert() to get the Alert interface, then: accept() (OK), dismiss() (Cancel), getText() (alert message), sendKeys() (prompt input)','Use JavaScript to dismiss','Alerts block automation permanently'],answer:1,explanation:'Alert alert = driver.switchTo().alert(); String msg = alert.getText(); alert.accept(); For prompts: alert.sendKeys("answer"); alert.accept(). Wrap in try/catch for conditional alerts. Use explicit wait alertIsPresent() before switching.'},
{id:4000,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you switch to an iframe in Selenium?',options:['iframes are not supported','driver.switchTo().frame(index), switchTo().frame("name"), or switchTo().frame(webElement). Return to main: switchTo().defaultContent() or switchTo().parentFrame()','Using JavaScript only','Using CSS selectors only'],answer:1,explanation:'switchTo().frame(0) for first frame. switchTo().frame("frameName") by name/id. switchTo().frame(driver.findElement(By.id("myFrame"))) by WebElement. All subsequent commands target the iframe DOM. switchTo().defaultContent() exits all frames.'},
{id:4001,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you handle multiple browser windows/tabs in Selenium?',options:['Only one window is supported','Get all handles: driver.getWindowHandles() returns Set<String>. Switch: driver.switchTo().window(handle). Store current: driver.getWindowHandle() to return later.','Use JavaScript window.open()','Use separate driver instances'],answer:1,explanation:'String main = driver.getWindowHandle(); driver.findElement(By.linkText("Open")).click(); for(String h : driver.getWindowHandles()){ if(!h.equals(main)) driver.switchTo().window(h); } // interact; driver.switchTo().window(main)'},
{id:4002,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `driver.manage().cookies()` used for?',options:['HTTP cookie parser','Manages browser cookies: addCookie(cookie), getCookieNamed(name), getCookies(), deleteCookieNamed(name), deleteAllCookies(). Useful for auth testing and state management.','Session storage access','Local storage access'],answer:1,explanation:'Cookies for authentication bypass: driver.manage().addCookie(new Cookie("sessionId","abc123")); driver.navigate().refresh() — bypasses login form. getCookies() for cookie assertions. deleteAllCookies() for cleanup.'},
{id:4003,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you take a screenshot in Selenium?',options:['Use the OS screenshot tool','Cast driver to TakesScreenshot: File f = ((TakesScreenshot)driver).getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE); FileUtils.copyFile(f, new File("screenshot.png"))','Only via JavascriptExecutor','Use a third-party library only'],answer:1,explanation:'TakesScreenshot: OutputType.FILE (saves to temp file), OutputType.BASE64 (base64 string), OutputType.BYTES (byte array). WebElement.getScreenshotAs() for element-only screenshots (Selenium 4). Embed bytes in Cucumber/Allure reports.'},
{id:4004,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `element.isDisplayed()` used for?',options:['Checks if element exists in DOM','Returns true if the element is visible (rendered and takes up space on screen) — checks display:none, visibility:hidden, opacity:0, size:0','Checks if element is enabled','Checks if element is selected'],answer:1,explanation:'isDisplayed() checks CSS visibility — returns false for display:none or visibility:hidden. Different from presenceOfElementLocated (which returns true even for hidden elements). isEnabled(): form field not disabled. isSelected(): checkbox/radio state.'},
{id:4005,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a CSS selector advantage over XPath in Selenium?',options:['XPath is always better','CSS selectors are generally faster to execute and more readable: #id, .class, div > p, [attribute=value]. XPath is more powerful (can traverse up to parent, use text(), etc.)','CSS selectors are deprecated','They have identical performance'],answer:1,explanation:'CSS: #userId (id), .btn-primary (class), input[type=submit] (attribute), div > span (direct child), p + span (adjacent sibling). Generally 20-30% faster than XPath in browsers. Use XPath when you need parent traversal or text() functions.'},
{id:4006,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What are XPath axes in Selenium?',options:['Coordinate system','XPath navigation directions: parent::, child::, following-sibling::, preceding-sibling::, ancestor::, descendant::, following::, preceding::, self::','Graph algorithms','DOM levels'],answer:1,explanation:'//label[text()="Username"]/following-sibling::input finds the input after the label. //td[contains(text(),"Alice")]/../td[2] gets sibling cell. XPath axes enable navigation that CSS selectors cannot — traversing up the DOM tree.'},
{id:4007,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What are XPath functions commonly used in Selenium?',options:['Only text()','contains(), starts-with(), ends-with(), text(), normalize-space(), last(), position(), count(), string-length(), translate(), not()','Only contains()','Math functions only'],answer:1,explanation:'contains(text(),"Login") — partial text match. normalize-space() — ignores extra whitespace. starts-with(@class,"btn") — partial class match. //button[last()] — last button. //tr[position()>1] — skip header row.'},
{id:4008,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `WebDriverManager` library?',options:['A browser manager','A library (io.github.bonigarcia:webdrivermanager) that automatically downloads, caches, and configures the correct browser driver binary for Selenium tests: WebDriverManager.chromedriver().setup()','A Selenium Grid manager','A test manager'],answer:1,explanation:'Without WebDriverManager, you must manually download ChromeDriver matching Chrome version and set System.setProperty. WebDriverManager: WebDriverManager.chromedriver().setup() auto-detects Chrome version and downloads matching driver.'},
{id:4009,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `ChromeOptions` used for in Selenium?',options:['Chrome browser settings API','A class for configuring Chrome launch options: addArguments("--headless"), addArguments("--no-sandbox"), setExperimentalOption, addExtensions, setDownloadPath','A Selenium configuration class','A capability class only'],answer:1,explanation:'ChromeOptions opts = new ChromeOptions(); opts.addArguments("--headless","--disable-gpu","--window-size=1920,1080"); opts.addArguments("--no-sandbox","--disable-dev-shm-usage"); driver = new ChromeDriver(opts). Essential for CI/CD headless runs.'},
{id:4010,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait()` vs `pageLoadTimeout()` vs `scriptTimeout()`?',options:['They are the same','implicitlyWait: time to find elements. pageLoadTimeout: time for page to load. scriptTimeout: time for executeAsyncScript to complete. Three independent timeouts.','Only implicitlyWait exists','pageLoadTimeout is deprecated'],answer:1,explanation:'Three timeouts: implicitlyWait(Duration.ofSeconds(10)) for element finding. pageLoadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30)) for page navigation. scriptTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10)) for async JS. Set independently based on application behavior.'},
{id:4011,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `element.clear()` used for?',options:['Deletes the element from DOM','Clears the current value of an input or textarea element, then triggers input/change events. Use before sendKeys() when field may have pre-filled content.','Removes all CSS classes','Clears browser cache'],answer:1,explanation:'clear() then sendKeys() ensures a clean input: el.clear(); el.sendKeys("new value"). clear() triggers input events and is more reliable than selectAll+delete for most form fields. Does not work on non-editable fields.'},
{id:4012,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between `findElement` and `findElements`?',options:['They are the same','findElement: returns first matching WebElement or throws NoSuchElementException. findElements: returns List<WebElement> (empty list if none — no exception). Use findElements to check existence.','findElements throws exception','findElement returns a list'],answer:1,explanation:'Check element existence safely: boolean exists = !driver.findElements(By.id("error")).isEmpty(). findElement throws if not found. findElements never throws — returns empty list. findElements().size() gives count.'},
{id:4013,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `By.cssSelector` vs `By.xpath` performance in Selenium?',options:['XPath is always faster','CSS selectors execute faster in most browsers since they leverage the browser\'s native CSS engine. XPath requires a separate interpreter. Prefer CSS unless you need XPath-specific features.','They have identical performance','CSS is deprecated'],answer:1,explanation:'Benchmark results typically show CSS 20-30% faster. CSS engine is highly optimized in browsers (used for rendering). XPath runs via an interpreter. Use CSS for simple selections, XPath when needing text() or parent traversal.'},
{id:4014,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the `Selenium IDE` tool?',options:['The main Selenium IDE','A browser extension for recording and replaying Selenium tests. Generates Selenium code in multiple languages. Good for quick prototyping but generated code often needs refactoring.','A standalone IDE','A test management tool'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium IDE (browser extension) records user actions and generates WebDriver code. Supports export to Java, Python, JavaScript, etc. Good for learning Selenium and quick test prototyping. Playback can be flaky for complex scenarios.'},
{id:4015,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `driver.getTitle()` and `driver.getCurrentUrl()`?',options:['Browser metadata methods','getTitle() returns the current page title (document.title). getCurrentUrl() returns the current URL including query parameters and fragments. Both useful for assertions.','Navigation methods','History methods'],answer:1,explanation:'Assert.assertEquals(driver.getTitle(), "Dashboard"); Assert.assertTrue(driver.getCurrentUrl().contains("/dashboard")). Useful for verifying navigation occurred correctly. Both reflect the currently loaded page.'},
{id:4016,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `element.submit()` in Selenium?',options:['A deprecated click method','Submits the form containing the element (equivalent to pressing Enter on a form field). Works on any element inside a form. Deprecated in Selenium 4 — use .click() on the submit button.','A form validator','An AJAX submitter'],answer:1,explanation:'element.submit() submits the parent form. In Selenium 4 it is deprecated — find and click the actual submit button explicitly. This makes tests more realistic (buttons can have onClick handlers) and reduces confusion.'},
{id:4017,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the `window_handles` pattern for new tabs/windows?',options:['Not needed','Store window handles before and after action: Set<String> before = driver.getWindowHandles(); click(); Set<String> after = driver.getWindowHandles(); after.removeAll(before) gives the new handle.','Use JavaScript window.open','Use driver.getNewTab()'],answer:1,explanation:'Pattern: Set<String> before = driver.getWindowHandles(); element.click(); Set<String> after = new HashSet<>(driver.getWindowHandles()); after.removeAll(before); String newWin = after.iterator().next(); driver.switchTo().window(newWin)'},
{id:4018,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `driver.manage().logs()` in Selenium?',options:['Returns test logs','Retrieves browser logs: driver.manage().logs().get(LogType.BROWSER) returns LogEntries with browser console messages (JavaScript errors, console.log)','A debugging API','A driver log accessor'],answer:1,explanation:'LogEntries logs = driver.manage().logs().get(LogType.BROWSER); for(LogEntry e : logs){ if(e.getLevel().equals(Level.SEVERE)) errors.add(e.getMessage()); }. Useful for detecting JS errors during test execution.'},
{id:4019,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you handle file uploads in Selenium?',options:['Click the upload button','Use sendKeys with the file path on a file input element: driver.findElement(By.cssSelector("input[type=file]")).sendKeys("/path/to/file.txt"). No need to click Browse.','Use Robot class only','Automation tools cannot handle file uploads'],answer:1,explanation:'sendKeys on input[type=file] directly sets the file path without opening the OS file dialog. Works cross-platform. For drag-and-drop file upload: use JavaScript or a robot framework. Absolute path required.'},
{id:4020,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you configure file downloads in Chrome via Selenium?',options:['Downloads cannot be automated','Set Chrome preferences: Map<String,Object> prefs = new HashMap<>(); prefs.put("download.default_directory", downloadPath); prefs.put("download.prompt_for_download", false); options.setExperimentalOption("prefs", prefs)','Use AutoIT only','Click the download button repeatedly'],answer:1,explanation:'Chrome preferences disable the download dialog and set directory. For headless Chrome: also add prefs.put("plugins.always_open_pdf_externally", true). Wait for file to appear using polling: while(!file.exists()) Thread.sleep(500).'},
{id:4021,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `element.getLocation()` and `element.getSize()`?',options:['Browser location methods','getLocation() returns Point(x,y) of element top-left corner. getSize() returns Dimension(width,height). Together used for element position/size assertions in visual testing.','Screen coordinate methods','WebDriver location methods'],answer:1,explanation:'Point p = element.getLocation() — x,y coordinates relative to viewport top-left. Dimension d = element.getSize() — width and height. element.getRect() (Selenium 4) returns both as Rectangle in one call.'},
{id:4022,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does `By.linkText("Click here")` vs `By.partialLinkText("Click")` do?',options:['They are identical','linkText: exact match of the anchor element\'s visible text. partialLinkText: partial/substring match. Both only work on <a> elements.','partialLinkText is deprecated','linkText finds by ID'],answer:1,explanation:'By.linkText("Sign in") requires exact visible text match (case-sensitive). By.partialLinkText("Sign") finds any anchor containing "Sign". Useful when link text is dynamic or very long. Less reliable than id/css — prefer those.'},
{id:4023,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the `Robot` class used for in Selenium tests?',options:['A Selenium component','Java\'s java.awt.Robot simulates OS-level keyboard/mouse events, used for handling OS-level dialogs (Windows file dialog, authentication popup) that Selenium cannot reach','An Android testing tool','A browser robot'],answer:1,explanation:'Robot robot = new Robot(); robot.keyPress(KeyEvent.VK_ENTER); robot.keyRelease(KeyEvent.VK_ENTER). Handles Windows file upload dialogs, OS authentication dialogs. Platform-specific — brittle across OSes. Prefer Selenium approaches when possible.'},
{id:4024,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `Keys.chord()` in Selenium?',options:['A music keyboard shortcut','Creates a special string containing multiple key presses to be sent simultaneously: Keys.chord(Keys.CONTROL,"a") selects all text, Keys.chord(Keys.CONTROL,"c") copies','A key combination logger','A keyboard event'],answer:1,explanation:'element.sendKeys(Keys.chord(Keys.CONTROL, Keys.SHIFT, "j")) — Ctrl+Shift+J. element.sendKeys(Keys.chord(Keys.CONTROL,"a"), Keys.DELETE) — select all then delete. Enables keyboard shortcuts in test automation.'},
{id:4025,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `element.getAttribute("innerHTML")` vs `getAttribute("textContent")`?',options:['They are identical','innerHTML returns the HTML markup inside the element including nested tags. textContent returns all text content including hidden text. Both are HTML properties accessed via getAttribute.','textContent is deprecated','innerHTML is not supported'],answer:1,explanation:'getAttribute("innerHTML") → "<b>Hello</b> <span>World</span>". getAttribute("textContent") → "Hello World". For user-visible text: getText(). For full markup inspection: innerHTML. textContent includes hidden element text.'},
{id:4026,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `WebDriverWait` `until()` with a custom `ExpectedCondition`?',options:['Only built-in conditions work','until() accepts any ExpectedCondition<T> lambda or anonymous class, enabling custom wait conditions beyond the built-in ExpectedConditions','A JUnit assertion helper','A retry mechanism'],answer:1,explanation:'wait.until(d -> d.findElements(By.css(".item")).size() >= 5) — waits until 5 items appear. wait.until(d -> !d.findElement(By.id("loading")).isDisplayed()) — wait for spinner to disappear. Lambda syntax since Java 8.'},
{id:4027,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you scroll to an element in Selenium?',options:['Only scrollIntoView JS works','Options: 1) JavascriptExecutor: executeScript("arguments[0].scrollIntoView(true);", element). 2) Actions: actions.moveToElement(element).perform() (also scrolls). 3) Selenium 4: new Actions(driver).scrollToElement(element).perform()','Selenium auto-scrolls always','Use Tab key to navigate'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium 4 W3C scroll API: new Actions(driver).scrollToElement(element).perform() — preferred. JS: executeScript("arguments[0].scrollIntoView({behavior:\'smooth\', block:\'center\'});", el). Actions.moveToElement triggers scroll as side effect.'},
{id:4028,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `driver.manage().timeouts().pageLoadTimeout()`?',options:['Time to find elements','The maximum time to wait for a page navigation to complete before throwing a TimeoutException. Default is 300 seconds (5 minutes).','Implicit wait timeout','Script execution timeout'],answer:1,explanation:'driver.manage().timeouts().pageLoadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30)). If page does not load in 30s: TimeoutException. Tune based on your application speed. Too short causes flaky tests on slow CI; too long hides real performance issues.'},
{id:4029,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `element.getRect()` in Selenium 4?',options:['A deprecated method','Returns a Rectangle containing x, y position and width, height dimensions in a single call, combining getLocation() and getSize()','A visual bounds method','A coordinate transformer'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium 4: Rectangle rect = element.getRect(); rect.x, rect.y, rect.width, rect.height. Single W3C API call instead of two (getLocation + getSize). More efficient for visual positioning assertions.'},
{id:4030,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does `driver.findElement(By.xpath("//table//tr[td[text()=\'Alice\']]"))` do?',options:['Finds the table only','Finds a table row (<tr>) that contains a cell (<td>) with exactly the text "Alice"','Finds a cell by index','Finds all rows'],answer:1,explanation:'XPath predicate with nested condition: //tr[td[text()="Alice"]] — find tr that has a descendant td whose text is "Alice". Useful for table row lookup by content. Add [1] for first match if multiple exist.'},
{id:4031,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a headless browser in Selenium testing?',options:['A browser with no CSS','A browser running without a graphical user interface, executing JavaScript and rendering pages in memory. Faster, no display required — ideal for CI/CD servers.','A browser without JavaScript','A lightweight browser'],answer:1,explanation:'Chrome headless: ChromeOptions opts = new ChromeOptions(); opts.addArguments("--headless=new","--disable-gpu"). Firefox headless: FirefoxOptions opts = new FirefoxOptions(); opts.addArguments("-headless"). No display server required. Tests run 30-40% faster.'},
{id:4032,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between Selenium 3 and Selenium 4?',options:['They are identical','Selenium 4: full W3C WebDriver compliance (no JSONWire Protocol), CDP support, relative locators, new window/tab API, Selenium Grid 4 with improved architecture, and BiDi API','Selenium 4 is slower','Selenium 3 is recommended'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium 4 key changes: W3C protocol only (no legacy JSONWire), Chrome DevTools Protocol access, relative locators (above/below/near/toLeftOf/toRightOf), newWindow(WindowType.TAB), improved Selenium Grid 4 with observability.'},
{id:4033,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What are relative locators in Selenium 4?',options:['Relative XPath expressions','Spatial locators that find elements relative to other elements: above(), below(), near(), toLeftOf(), toRightOf(). Example: RelativeLocator.with(By.tagName("input")).above(submitButton)','Dynamic locators','Position-based locators'],answer:1,explanation:'RelativeLocator.with(By.tagName("td")).toRightOf(driver.findElement(By.id("userLabel"))) finds the table cell to the right of the user label. Useful when elements lack unique identifiers but have consistent spatial relationships.'},
{id:4034,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is Selenium Grid 4?',options:['An outdated version','A completely rewritten distributed testing infrastructure supporting: Standalone (one node), Hub-Node (traditional), Fully Distributed, and Kubernetes modes with improved observability and dynamic registration','A deprecated product','A Docker container'],answer:1,explanation:'Grid 4 uses OpenTelemetry, gRPC, and HTTP/2. Standalone: java -jar selenium-server.jar standalone. Node can register dynamically. Supports Docker nodes, auto-download of browser drivers. Built-in observability dashboard.'},
{id:4035,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `RemoteWebDriver` in Selenium?',options:['A local driver','A WebDriver implementation that connects to a remote Selenium server (Grid, BrowserStack, Sauce Labs) via HTTP: new RemoteWebDriver(new URL("http://hub:4444/wd/hub"), options)','A virtual driver','An abstract driver'],answer:1,explanation:'RemoteWebDriver sends WebDriver commands over HTTP to a remote node instead of a local browser. Required for Selenium Grid and cloud providers. Accepts URL and capabilities/options. The browser runs on the remote machine.'},
{id:4036,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What are `DesiredCapabilities` and why are they deprecated in Selenium 4?',options:['A configuration class still recommended','A map of browser capabilities previously used with RemoteWebDriver. Deprecated in Selenium 4 — replaced by browser-specific Options classes (ChromeOptions, FirefoxOptions) which are W3C compliant.','A mandatory Selenium 4 class','A server configuration'],answer:1,explanation:'DesiredCapabilities was a generic map and did not enforce W3C format. Selenium 4 uses Options classes: ChromeOptions, FirefoxOptions, EdgeOptions, SafariOptions. These generate valid W3C capability JSON automatically.'},
{id:4037,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP) access in Selenium 4?',options:['A Chrome extension API','Selenium 4 provides direct CDP access via HasDevTools interface, enabling network interception, console monitoring, geolocation override, performance monitoring, and more','A debug tool only','A Selenium Grid feature'],answer:1,explanation:'ChromeDriver cd = (ChromeDriver)driver; DevTools dt = cd.getDevTools(); dt.createSession(); dt.send(Network.enable(Optional.empty(),Optional.empty(),Optional.empty())); dt.addListener(Network.requestWillBeSent(),r->log(r.getRequest().getUrl()));'},
{id:4038,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you intercept network requests with Selenium 4 CDP?',options:['Not possible','Use DevTools Network domain: enable network, addListener for requestWillBeSent and responseReceived events, or use Network.setRequestInterception for blocking/modifying requests','Use a proxy only','Use JavaScript fetch override'],answer:1,explanation:'dt.send(Network.enable()); dt.addListener(Network.responseReceived(), r->{ if(r.getResponse().getStatus()==404) failures.add(r.getResponse().getUrl()); }). Network interception also available via Selenium BiDi API in Selenium 4.'},
{id:4039,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `driver.switchTo().newWindow(WindowType.TAB)` in Selenium 4?',options:['Opens a new browser','Opens a new browser tab (WindowType.TAB) or window (WindowType.WINDOW) and automatically switches the WebDriver focus to it. New in Selenium 4.','Switches to an existing tab','Duplicates current tab'],answer:1,explanation:'driver.switchTo().newWindow(WindowType.TAB) — opens blank tab, driver focused on it. driver.switchTo().newWindow(WindowType.WINDOW) — opens new window. Replaces the previous workaround of JavascriptExecutor window.open().'},
{id:4040,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Selenium BiDi (Bidirectional) API?',options:['A two-driver setup','A new bidirectional communication protocol (WebDriver BiDi) allowing Selenium to receive real-time events from browsers (console logs, network events, JS exceptions) without polling','A parallel execution API','A REST API layer'],answer:1,explanation:'BiDi enables event-driven testing: subscribe to console logs, intercept network requests in real-time, receive JS exceptions immediately. Implemented as part of W3C WebDriver BiDi standard. Available in Selenium 4.3+ with supporting browsers.'},
{id:4041,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is cross-browser testing with Selenium?',options:['Testing on two browsers only','Running the same test suite across multiple browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari) to verify consistent behavior, achieved via parameterized test configuration or Selenium Grid','A Selenium Grid feature only','A cloud-only practice'],answer:1,explanation:'Cross-browser testing: parameterize browser choice via TestNG @DataProvider or system property. Use Selenium Grid for parallel cross-browser execution. BrowserStack/Sauce Labs provide cloud browser matrices without local installation.'},
{id:4042,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is BrowserStack Automate and how does Selenium integrate?',options:['A browser download service','A cloud testing platform providing real browsers and devices. Integrate via RemoteWebDriver with BrowserStack hub URL and capabilities including bs:options for session name, build, project.','A local browser emulator','A Selenium IDE cloud version'],answer:1,explanation:'RemoteWebDriver(new URL("https://USERNAME:ACCESSKEY@hub.browserstack.com/wd/hub"), options). bs:options: { "buildName":"CI-Build", "sessionName":"Login Test", "debug":true, "networkLogs":true }. Access video recordings and logs in BrowserStack dashboard.'},
{id:4043,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is Selenium with Sauce Labs?',options:['A food delivery app','A cloud testing platform for Selenium/Appium tests. Connect via RemoteWebDriver to ondemand.us-west-1.saucelabs.com with sauce:options capabilities including username, accessKey, tunnelName.','A Selenium Grid alternative only','A test management tool'],answer:1,explanation:'Sauce Labs provides 900+ browser/OS combinations. sauce:options: { "username":"user","accessKey":"key","build":"sprint-42","name":"Login Test" }. Sauce Connect Proxy for testing against localhost. Extended debugging, video, logs available.'},
{id:4044,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is LambdaTest for Selenium?',options:['A functional testing library','A cloud-based cross-browser testing platform with 3000+ browser/OS combinations. Integrate via RemoteWebDriver with hub.lambdatest.com and LT:Options capabilities.','A Lambda testing tool','A performance testing platform'],answer:1,explanation:'LambdaTest: RemoteWebDriver(new URL("https://user:key@hub.lambdatest.com/wd/hub"), options). LT:Options: { "build":"Sprint-1","name":"Test","browserName":"Chrome","version":"latest","platformName":"Windows 10" }. Live testing, automated, geolocation testing.'},
{id:4045,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is Selenium with TestNG integration pattern?',options:['Selenium is TestNG-specific','TestNG runner with @BeforeMethod (driver init) and @AfterMethod (driver quit) + ThreadLocal<WebDriver> for parallel execution. @DataProvider for cross-browser parameterization.','JUnit is required','Only JUnit works with Selenium'],answer:1,explanation:'Pattern: ThreadLocal<WebDriver> driver = new ThreadLocal<>(); @BeforeMethod(alwaysRun=true) void setup(){ driver.set(new ChromeDriver()); } @AfterMethod(alwaysRun=true) void quit(){ driver.get().quit(); driver.remove(); }'},
{id:4046,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is ThreadLocal<WebDriver> and why is it essential for parallel Selenium execution?',options:['A thread pool for drivers','ThreadLocal provides each thread with its own WebDriver instance, preventing tests running in parallel from sharing/interfering with each other\'s browser','A driver caching mechanism','A driver pool'],answer:1,explanation:'Without ThreadLocal: parallel tests share one driver — race conditions, wrong page state, test interference. ThreadLocal<WebDriver>: each TestNG/JUnit thread has its own driver. Essential for parallel=methods or parallel=classes in TestNG.'},
{id:4047,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a base test class pattern in Selenium?',options:['An abstract test class','An abstract class containing driver setup/teardown and helper methods (getDriver, waitForElement, takeScreenshot) that all page-specific test classes extend, centralizing WebDriver lifecycle','A TestNG base class','A Spring configuration class'],answer:1,explanation:'abstract class BaseTest { ThreadLocal<WebDriver> driver; @BeforeMethod void setup(){ driver.set(new ChromeDriver()); } @AfterMethod void quit(){ driver.get().quit(); } protected WebDriver getDriver(){ return driver.get(); } }'},
{id:4048,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Selenium test framework architecture (Page Object + Base + Utilities)?',options:['A single-class approach','Layered: tests (assertions/flow) → page objects (element+actions) → base classes (driver lifecycle) → utilities (waits, screenshots, reporting). Separation of concerns.','All in one class','MVC pattern only'],answer:1,explanation:'src/test: tests/ (test classes), pages/ (page objects), base/ (BaseTest, BasePage), utils/ (WaitHelper, ScreenshotUtil, ReportUtil), config/ (ConfigReader, DriverFactory). Each layer has one responsibility.'},
{id:4049,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a DriverFactory in Selenium?',options:['A WebDriver builder','A factory class/method that creates the appropriate WebDriver based on configuration (browser type, environment): if("chrome") return new ChromeDriver(); if("firefox") return new FirefoxDriver()','A Selenium Grid component','A browser launcher'],answer:1,explanation:'DriverFactory encapsulates driver creation: reads browser from config/system property, applies options (headless, proxy, etc.), returns configured driver. Tests do not need to know browser-specific setup details.'},
{id:4050,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Selenium anti-pattern: using `Thread.sleep()`?',options:['An accepted practice','Thread.sleep() is a fixed delay that makes tests slow and brittle. Use explicit waits (WebDriverWait + ExpectedConditions) which adapt to actual element state, reducing wait times and preventing flakiness.','A Selenium feature','An optimization technique'],answer:1,explanation:'Thread.sleep(3000) always waits 3 seconds even if element appears in 100ms. On slow CI: 3s may not be enough. Use: wait.until(ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(By.id("btn"))). Adapts dynamically to element state.'},
{id:4051,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Selenium anti-pattern: brittle XPath?',options:['Fast XPath usage','XPaths like //div[3]/table[1]/tr[2]/td[5] break with any DOM structure change. Use meaningful attributes: //button[@data-testid="submit"] or //input[@name="username"].','XPath performance tuning','A test anti-pattern unrelated to Selenium'],answer:1,explanation:'Positional XPaths (//td[3]) break when rows/columns are reordered. Absolute XPaths (/html/body/div[1]/...) break on any DOM change. Use: id, data-testid, name, aria-label, meaningful CSS selectors — stable across refactors.'},
{id:4052,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Selenium anti-pattern: no Page Object Model?',options:['A valid alternative','Writing all locators and interactions inline in test methods creates duplicated, unmaintainable code. When UI changes, you must update every test instead of just the page object.','A speed optimization','A testing style choice'],answer:1,explanation:'Without POM: driver.findElement(By.id("user")).sendKeys() duplicated in 50 tests. With POM: loginPage.enterUsername() — change locator once in LoginPage. Tests read as behavior descriptions, not UI interaction sequences.'},
{id:4053,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is Selenium with Docker?',options:['Selenium in a container only','Running Selenium Grid nodes as Docker containers using official selenium/node-chrome, selenium/node-firefox images or Selenium Grid 4 Docker Compose setup for scalable CI/CD browser testing','A Docker plugin','A Docker desktop extension'],answer:1,explanation:'docker run -d -p 4444:4444 selenium/standalone-chrome. Or Docker Compose: selenium/hub + selenium/node-chrome + selenium/node-firefox. Grid 4 also supports dynamic node scaling with docker.dynamicGridConfig.'},
{id:4054,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is Selenium with GitHub Actions?',options:['GitHub Actions ships with Selenium','Run Chrome headless in GitHub Actions: ubuntu-latest runner has Chrome pre-installed. Setup: actions/setup-java, then mvn test with -Dheadless=true or Chrome options.addArguments("--headless=new").','A separate GitHub product','Requires a special runner'],answer:1,explanation:'GitHub Actions ubuntu-latest includes Chrome and ChromeDriver. For ChromeDriver version management: use WebDriverManager or browser-driver-installer action. Add --no-sandbox --disable-dev-shm-usage for Docker/CI compatibility.'},
{id:4055,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `--disable-dev-shm-usage` Chrome argument and why is it needed in CI?',options:['A performance optimization only','Disables the use of /dev/shm (shared memory) which is often small in Docker containers. Without it, Chrome crashes in containers due to insufficient shared memory for rendering.','A security setting','An accessibility option'],answer:1,explanation:'Docker containers default /dev/shm to 64MB — Chrome needs more for rendering. --disable-dev-shm-usage makes Chrome use /tmp instead. Combined with --no-sandbox (for non-root containers), these two args are standard for CI.'},
{id:4056,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `--no-sandbox` Chrome argument in Selenium?',options:['Disables JavaScript sandboxing','Disables Chrome\'s sandboxing mechanism required for running Chrome as root user in containers. Only use in controlled CI/Docker environments — not in production browsers.','A security enhancement','A performance flag'],answer:1,explanation:'Chrome requires non-root user for sandboxing. In Docker containers running as root: --no-sandbox bypasses this requirement. Security risk in untrusted environments. Standard CI args: --headless=new --no-sandbox --disable-dev-shm-usage.'},
{id:4057,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is proxy configuration in Selenium?',options:['Not supported','Set proxy via BrowserOptions: ChromeOptions opts = new ChromeOptions(); Proxy proxy = new Proxy(); proxy.setHttpProxy("proxy.host:8080"); opts.setCapability("proxy", proxy)','Only system proxy is supported','Use environment variables only'],answer:1,explanation:'Proxy proxy = new Proxy(); proxy.setHttpProxy("host:port"); proxy.setSslProxy("host:port"); options.setCapability(CapabilityType.PROXY, proxy). For corporate proxies, Selenium tests must route through proxy. BrowserMob Proxy or Charles for interception.'},
{id:4058,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is SSL certificate handling in Selenium?',options:['SSL breaks Selenium always','Set acceptInsecureCerts capability to bypass SSL certificate errors: options.setAcceptInsecureCerts(true). Allows testing on self-signed certificate environments.','SSL is automatically trusted','Use JavaScript to bypass SSL'],answer:1,explanation:'options.setAcceptInsecureCerts(true) is a W3C WebDriver capability. Equivalent to clicking "Advanced > Proceed to site anyway". Needed for test environments with self-signed or expired certificates. Not recommended for production URLs.'},
{id:4059,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Selenium screenshot on failure pattern with TestNG listener?',options:['Manual screenshot only','Implement ITestListener.onTestFailure(): cast driver to TakesScreenshot, capture screenshot, save to file with test name and timestamp, optionally embed in report','A Selenium built-in feature','Only works with JUnit'],answer:1,explanation:'ITestListener: public void onTestFailure(ITestResult r){ Object inst=r.getInstance(); WebDriver d=((BaseTest)inst).getDriver(); File f=((TakesScreenshot)d).getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE); FileUtils.copyFile(f,new File("fail-"+r.getName()+".png")); }'},
{id:4060,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Page Object Model "fluent interface" pattern?',options:['Using Java streams','Page object methods return `this` (the page object) allowing chained calls: loginPage.enterUsername("user").enterPassword("pass").clickLogin() returns the next page object.','A Selenium feature','A testing anti-pattern'],answer:1,explanation:'loginPage.enterUsername("alice").enterPassword("pass123").clickLogin() returns DashboardPage. DashboardPage extends BasePage. Readable test code that mirrors user workflow. Chain breaks on error — debugging can be harder.'},
{id:4061,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is Selenium Grid Hub and Node architecture?',options:['Hub and Node are the same','Hub: central coordinator that receives WebDriver requests and routes to Nodes. Nodes: machines with browsers that register with Hub and execute tests. Enables parallel cross-browser execution.','A single machine setup','A cloud configuration only'],answer:1,explanation:'Grid 3: java -jar selenium-server.jar -role hub; java -jar selenium-server.jar -role node -hub http://hub:4444/grid/register. Grid 4: java -jar selenium-server.jar hub; java -jar selenium-server.jar node --hub http://hub:4444. Nodes register automatically.'},
{id:4062,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Selenium Grid 4 standalone mode?',options:['Grid without nodes','A single process running both Hub and Node functionality — simplest setup for single-machine parallel testing: java -jar selenium-server.jar standalone','A Selenium 3 feature','Grid without a hub'],answer:1,explanation:'Standalone mode: one JAR runs everything. Supports multiple browsers on one machine. Start: java -jar selenium-server.jar standalone. Access UI: http://localhost:4444. Nodes = local browser instances. No separate node process needed.'},
{id:4063,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is Selenium Grid 4 fully distributed mode?',options:['Another name for standalone','Separates all Grid components into independent services: Router, Distributor, Session Map, Node(s), Event Bus — each runs as a separate process. Enables Kubernetes deployment and elastic scaling.','A deprecated mode','A Docker-only mode'],answer:1,explanation:'Components: Event Bus (pub/sub), Session Map (session storage), Distributor (allocates sessions), Router (routes requests), Node (browser). Each scales independently. Ideal for large Kubernetes-based Grid deployments.'},
{id:4064,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Screenplay pattern in Selenium?',options:['A scripting pattern','An actor-centric design pattern (from SOLID principles) where Actors perform Tasks and ask Questions about the UI. More expressive than POM for complex workflows: actor.attemptsTo(Navigate.to(url), Click.on(button))','A TestNG pattern','A Selenium 4 feature'],answer:1,explanation:'Screenplay: Actor.named("Alice").can(BrowseTheWeb.with(driver)).attemptsTo(Click.on(LoginButton)). Serenity BDD implements Screenplay. More composable than POM — Tasks combine smaller interactions. Follows open/closed principle.'},
{id:4065,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the Serenity BDD + Selenium combination?',options:['A Selenium plugin','Serenity BDD wraps Selenium providing: automatic screenshots, step-level reporting, @Steps injection, Screenplay pattern support, REST Assured integration, and rich living documentation HTML reports','A Cucumber tool only','A reporting addon'],answer:1,explanation:'Serenity: add serenity-core and serenity-junit5/serenity-testng. Use @Managed WebDriver injection: @Managed WebDriver driver. Steps logged automatically. Run: mvn verify → generates target/site/serenity/index.html with full test narrative.'},
{id:4066,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `element.getCssValue("property")` in Selenium?',options:['Gets HTML style attribute','Returns the computed CSS property value of an element: element.getCssValue("background-color") returns "rgba(255, 0, 0, 1)". Useful for visual assertions.','Gets inline style only','Returns the CSS class name'],answer:1,explanation:'getCssValue("color") returns computed color (rgba format). getCssValue("font-size") returns "16px". getCssValue("display") returns "block"/"none". Useful for asserting styling in tests, though visual regression tools are more comprehensive.'},
{id:4067,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the `@CacheLookup` annotation in PageFactory?',options:['A performance annotation','Tells PageFactory to cache the WebElement after first lookup instead of re-finding it on each access. Only safe for static, never-re-rendered elements.','A required annotation','An obsolete annotation'],answer:1,explanation:'@CacheLookup @FindBy(id="header") WebElement header — caches after first find. If the page or element is ever refreshed/re-rendered, cached reference becomes stale. Only use for truly static elements (headers, nav bars).'},
{id:4068,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you verify element does NOT exist in Selenium?',options:['driver.notExists(locator)','driver.findElements(By.locator) returns empty List if none found (no exception). Check: driver.findElements(By.id("error")).isEmpty() returns true if element not present.','Try/catch NoSuchElementException','element.isDisplayed() returns false'],answer:1,explanation:'boolean absent = driver.findElements(By.id("errorMsg")).isEmpty(). Or: boolean absent = !driver.findElements(By.id("errorMsg")).stream().anyMatch(WebElement::isDisplayed). findElements never throws — returns empty list for no matches.'},
{id:4069,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Selenium `@FindAll` annotation?',options:['Finds all elements','Marks a List<WebElement> field with multiple @FindBy locators that are combined with OR logic — the list contains all elements matching ANY of the locators','An any-match finder','A CSS and XPath combiner'],answer:1,explanation:'@FindAll({@FindBy(id="ok"),@FindBy(id="confirm")}) List<WebElement> acceptButtons — finds all elements matching id="ok" OR id="confirm". Different from @FindBys (AND logic). Returns all matches from all locators combined.'},
{id:4070,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does `driver.manage().deleteAllCookies()` do?',options:['Clears browser cache','Removes all cookies from the current domain for the active session — used in test teardown or before each test to ensure clean browser state','Deletes session storage','Clears local storage'],answer:1,explanation:'deleteAllCookies() in @BeforeMethod ensures no leftover auth cookies affect the next test. Useful for resetting login state. Note: only deletes cookies for the current domain. Combine with localStorage/sessionStorage clear via JS.'},
{id:4071,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `executeScript("localStorage.clear();")` used for?',options:['A CSS reset','Clears all localStorage entries for the current origin using JavaScript — useful for resetting application state between tests when the app uses localStorage','Deletes cookies','Clears session storage'],answer:1,explanation:'executeScript("localStorage.clear()") and executeScript("sessionStorage.clear()") reset web storage state. Use in @BeforeMethod with deleteAllCookies() for complete browser state reset between tests.'},
{id:4072,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `Actions.scrollByAmount()` in Selenium 4?',options:['An element scroller','A W3C-compliant scroll action: new Actions(driver).scrollByAmount(deltaX, deltaY).perform() — scrolls by pixels from the current viewport position','A drag action','A keyboard scroll'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium 4 W3C Actions scroll: new Actions(driver).scrollByAmount(0, 500).perform() — scroll 500px down. scrollToElement(el) scrolls element into view. These use the W3C wheel action, more reliable than JS scrollTo.'},
{id:4073,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is Selenide, and how does it relate to Selenium?',options:['A Selenium version','A Fluent API wrapper around Selenium WebDriver (Java) that provides automatic waits, more concise syntax, and smart element finding: $(By.id("btn")).click() auto-waits for element to be visible','A Selenium plugin','A Selenium competitor'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenide: $("input#username").setValue("alice"); $(".btn-primary").click(); $(".error-msg").shouldBe(visible); Auto-waits remove the need for explicit WebDriverWait. Built-in screenshot on failure, report integration. Very popular in Java Selenium projects.'},
{id:4074,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is FluentSelenium?',options:['A Selenium fork','A wrapper library providing fluent/chainable API for WebDriver interactions with built-in retry logic and readable assertions. Less common than Selenide.','A JavaScript port','A deprecated library'],answer:1,explanation:'FluentSelenium: fluentWebDriver.input(By.id("user")).clearField().sendKeys("alice"); inOrder().element(By.css(".error")).within(secs(5)).isNotDisplayed(). Provides retry semantics and readable API on top of WebDriver.'},
{id:4075,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is Appium\'s inheritance from WebDriver?',options:['Appium is unrelated to Selenium','Appium implements the WebDriver protocol, so AppiumDriver extends RemoteWebDriver. All WebDriver commands (findElement, sendKeys, click) work on Appium. Mobile-specific interactions are added as extensions.','Appium replaces WebDriver','Appium uses a different protocol'],answer:1,explanation:'AppiumDriver extends RemoteWebDriver (Selenium). WebDriver methods work for mobile apps. Appium adds: mobile gestures (tap, swipe, pinch), mobile contexts (NATIVE_APP, WEBVIEW), device commands. Same Selenium API + mobile extensions.'},
{id:4076,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the `Dimension` class used for in Selenium?',options:['Screen dimensions only','Represents width and height: new Dimension(1920, 1080). Used with window.setSize() for viewport testing and element.getSize() return type.','A CSS measurement','A coordinate pair'],answer:1,explanation:'driver.manage().window().setSize(new Dimension(375, 812)) — sets iPhone X viewport. element.getSize() returns Dimension. Dimension.width, Dimension.height accessors. Used for responsive testing and visual assertions.'},
{id:4077,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `Point` class used for in Selenium?',options:['A geometry class','Represents x,y coordinates: used by element.getLocation() (returns Point), window.setPosition() (takes Point), and in Actions for precise click coordinates','A map marker','A coordinate transformer'],answer:1,explanation:'Point loc = element.getLocation() — top-left corner of element. driver.manage().window().setPosition(new Point(0,0)) — move window to top-left. new Actions(driver).moveByOffset(x,y).click().perform() uses coordinates.'},
{id:4078,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is `waitForCondition` with a lambda in Selenium 4?',options:['A deprecated API','WebDriverWait.until() with a Function<WebDriver,T> lambda: wait.until(d -> d.findElement(By.id("count")).getText().equals("5")) — custom conditions in one line','An ExpectedConditions method','A JUnit rule'],answer:1,explanation:'Lambda conditions: wait.until(d->!d.findElements(By.css(".spinner")).stream().anyMatch(WebElement::isDisplayed)) — wait for all spinners to disappear. Concise, no need for custom ExpectedCondition class.'},
{id:4079,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is Selenium with Spring Boot for integration testing?',options:['Spring Boot only for APIs','Use @SpringBootTest(webEnvironment=RANDOM_PORT) to start Spring Boot app on random port, then use WebDriver to test the actual running application via browser automation','Selenium does not work with Spring','Only JUnit 5 supports this'],answer:1,explanation:'@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment=RANDOM_PORT) + @LocalServerPort int port — Spring starts the real server. driver.get("http://localhost:"+port+"/login"). True integration test: real server + real browser. Slower but highest fidelity.'},
{id:4080,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is visual regression testing with Selenium?',options:['Testing CSS only','Capturing baseline screenshots and comparing subsequent runs pixel-by-pixel or perceptually to detect unintended visual changes. Tools: Applitools Eyes, Percy, BackstopJS, Ashot.','A performance test type','An accessibility test type'],answer:1,explanation:'Applitools: eyes.open(driver, "App", "Login Page"); eyes.checkWindow("Full page"); eyes.close(). Uses AI to compare. AShot: takes full-page screenshots with viewport scrolling. Percy: snapshot comparison in CI.'}
]);
